To handle WCAG 2.1 AA (4.5:1 contrast ratio) compliance in dark theme when brand colors have low luminance, Material 3 uses a **Tonal Palette** system. 

Instead of using a single static brand color across both themes, we map the brand color to a range of tones (from 0 to 100 luminance) and assign specific tones to specific roles depending on the active theme.

Here is how we handle this in practice for `UserSettingsView`:

### 1. Use Tonal Shifts (Tone 80 vs. Tone 40)
* **Light Theme**: The primary brand color is mapped to **Tone 40** (medium-dark, high contrast against light backgrounds).
* **Dark Theme**: We do *not* use the raw brand color for text or critical icons. Instead, we shift to **Tone 80** (a desaturated, lighter pastel version of the brand color). This ensures the color retains its brand hue but has high enough luminance to meet the 4.5:1 contrast ratio against dark backgrounds (typically Tone 10 or 12).

### 2. Leverage M3 Color Roles
We avoid using raw color values directly on text. Instead, we use semantic token roles:
* **`color: var(--md-sys-color-primary)`**: Resolves to Tone 40 in light mode, and Tone 80 in dark mode.
* **`background-color: var(--md-sys-color-primary-container)`**: Resolves to a soft, low-luminance container color (Tone 90 in light, Tone 30 in dark).
* **`color: var(--md-sys-color-on-primary-container)`**: Resolves to a highly contrasting text color designed to sit on top of that container (Tone 10 in light, Tone 90 in dark).

### 3. SCSS Implementation Strategy
In our SCSS token definition, we will define these mappings explicitly:

```scss
:root {
  // Light Theme (Default)
  --md-sys-color-primary: #0061a4; // Brand Blue (Tone 40) - 4.5:1+ on white
  --md-sys-color-on-primary: #ffffff;
  --md-sys-color-surface: #fcfcff;
  --md-sys-color-on-surface: #1a1c1e;
}

@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
  :root {
    // Dark Theme Override
    --md-sys-color-primary: #9ecaff; // Lightened Brand Blue (Tone 80) - 4.5:1+ on dark surface
    --md-sys-color-on-primary: #003258;
    --md-sys-color-surface: #1a1c1e; // Dark Surface (Tone 10)
    --md-sys-color-on-surface: #e2e2e6;
  }
}
```

### Proposed Update to the Specification
I will update the proposal's **Acceptance Criteria** to explicitly require the use of **M3 Tonal Palettes** (shifting brand colors to Tone 80/90 equivalents in dark mode) to guarantee WCAG AA compliance. 

